2.2 Representation requires non-substitution
Four of the five entries are straightforward. The fifth is not.
Self-models are routinely wrong about motives, capacities and emotional states. Because no model is a perfect copy of what it models, there must be a representable mismatch between model and reality. They are necessarily different things.
A self-model therefore requires two distinguishable informational roles: the representing state and the represented process. What does that require physically?
Not simultaneity. Signal transmission takes time, and interhemispheric transmission alone costs tens of milliseconds; a simultaneity criterion would exclude every real nervous system.
The requirement is non-substitution:
The representing state and the represented process must be co-instantiated in non-substituted substrate.
This defeats the obvious alternative, in which a single system alternates between running a process and modeling it. While the system is the model, the process is not running and there is nothing being modeled; while it is the process, no representation is held. The pair never exists. The objection is displacement, not lag.
A system meeting this condition contains a partition: two informational roles, co-instantiated, neither replacing the other. Communication follows rather than being assumed, since two roles that must be related require a channel between them.
The "other" is an informational role, not a modeled agent. The receiving pole must stand in a relation of non-identity to the source pole; nothing requires the system to represent that source as an entity or to hold a concept of other minds.
A correction, since earlier versions of this framework drew the wrong conclusion from that clause. Previous drafts closed this section by asserting that self-awareness in the sense of §1.1 is therefore a structural property of the architecture. That is inconsistent with the sentence preceding it and with §1.1's own definition, which specifies an explicit model of oneself as an entity embedded in relations. Nothing in the partition supplies an entity-representation.
What the partition supplies is the capacity to hold a representation of one's own running process — the view from inside. Modelling oneself as an entity, the view from outside, is a separate achievement with a separate source, and §2.8 treats it. The two were conflated for several versions, and the conflation concealed a failure mode the architecture in fact permits.
What the condition excludes. A single neuron is the clean negative case. Dendrite → soma → axon is a pipeline: representational format changes at each step, direction is fixed, and the poles are not exchangeable. A stage holds the transformed output of its predecessor, not a representation of it. The condition is weak, but it is not empty, and it returns the correct verdict at a scale where the wrong verdict would be conspicuous.
Self-models must remain revisable. The condition above establishes that model and reality can differ. It does not establish that the difference can be corrected, and a self-model region that evidence cannot reach is a distinct failure from a self-model that is merely wrong. Where a claim about the system's own state persists unchanged while contrary evidence accumulates, that region has become terminal, and the accumulating-evidence-without-movement signature is what identifies it. The anosognosias are the clinical instance (§3.4), and the framework's prediction there is unusual: awareness is intact and the self-model is locally unrevisable, which dissociates self-representation from phenomenality.
This condition is deliberately weak. A recurrent network with a delay line satisfies it, with current state as process and delay-line contents as representation. A stronger criterion — mutual information thresholds, non-overlapping state spaces — would either exclude real nervous systems or fail to exclude the trivial cases anyway. The roles may be realised anatomically, dynamically, or through segregated populations within one structure. Partition is a floor; the discriminating requirements are §2.3 and §2.4.
2.3 Two channel types
Partitions differ by the kind of channel that crosses them.
A symmetric channel carries the same representational format in both directions. Either pole may originate, the poles are exchangeable in principle, and each receives representations in its own native currency that it did not itself produce.
The poles must also be functionally comparable. Format and bidirectionality are not sufficient: two regions running different operations, connected by a bidirectional tract, form a processing chain rather than a peer partition, and a chain does not satisfy §2.2. A stage holds the transformed output of its predecessor, not a representation of it. What §2.2 requires is two instances of comparable process, either of which could stand as the representation of the other.
This is why callosal connections are the paradigm case. They are predominantly homotopic, linking the same functional area across hemispheres, so the two poles run comparable computations on different input. Long association tracts within a hemisphere — the arcuate fasciculus joining posterior and anterior language regions, for instance — are bidirectional and share a cortical format but connect functionally distinct regions, and are pipelines rather than peer channels.
An asymmetric channel carries different representational formats in each direction. The poles cannot exchange roles: one transduces environmental state into the system's internal format, the other transduces internal state into environmental effect.
Operationally the distinction is marked by invertibility. A symmetric channel's mapping is approximately invertible, since sender and receiver use a common code: cortical activity crossing the callosum can in principle be decoded back to the state that produced it. Transduction cannot — a spike train does not determine the photon flux that produced it. Where the mapping is non-invertible in both directions and lossy in different ways, the channel is asymmetric.
Retrograde traffic does not by itself establish symmetry, and the backpropagating action potential is the instructive near-miss. It travels from soma into dendrite, is essential to spike-timing-dependent plasticity, and is genuinely bidirectional. But it is the same signal on every occasion, carrying nothing about what was transmitted forward. It is a timing pulse, not a representation, and the invertibility criterion is what separates the two.
A symmetric channel supplies what §2.2 requires: a representation of the system, in the system's own format, originating outside the receiving process. It also makes narrative binding possible, since two streams can be unified only if they were separate streams in a comparable currency.
An asymmetric channel supplies three things a symmetric channel cannot.
Boundary. Where representational format changes kind is where inside meets outside. Two peers exchanging in a single format have no marked edge.
Constraint. Format-limited, finite input bounds the space of admissible internal states. Without it the generative pole free-runs: internally coherent, externally unmoored.
Valence. Homeostatic error is error relative to a boundary the system is maintaining, and therefore carries a sign intrinsically. Deviation from a defended setpoint is natively good or bad for the system. Peer-to-peer traffic contains no setpoint and generates no such quantity. Valence enters through the asymmetric limb or not at all.
A partition is cheap. A partition with a transductive limb closing a homeostatic loop is not.
Establishment and uptake
Valence is not a single event. It has two stages, they occur in different structures, and they fail separately.
Establishment. Homeostatic error is generated at the transductive surface, against a boundary the system maintains. This is where the sign comes from, and it requires no generative process at all — a decorticate body establishes homeostatic error continuously.
Uptake. Established error constrains the generative process: it narrows which internal states are subsequently occupied. Nothing is apprehended, inspected or read. Uptake is the error operating as constraint rather than as a signal sitting unused.
The reason for separating them is that §1.1 defines stakes as states being natively good or bad for it, and the "for it" requires an it. Establishment supplies the error. Uptake is what makes there be a system the error is for. A body whose homeostatic regulation is intact and whose generative structure is destroyed has error and no one it belongs to.
This is what constraint efficacy amounts to, and the condition therefore ceases to be an additional requirement bolted onto the asymmetric channel. Narrowing the space of occupiable internal states is uptake. An asymmetric channel is closed when both stages run; a channel delivering established error that constrains nothing has completed the first stage only.
The free-running failure named above is now specifiable. A generative pole receiving signal that does not constrain it has not lost its input — it has lost the second stage, which is the stage that makes it a generative structure at all (§2.6).
The decomposition sorts the clinical cases, which is the argument for it. Each of the three principal dissociations breaks a different stage or channel, and one vocabulary distinguishes them:
