The partition supplies the internal angle and nothing beyond it. §2.2 gives the system the capacity to hold a representation of its own running process. That is the whole of what the architecture delivers at this tier, and it is why §2.2's earlier claim to deliver §1.1 self-awareness was an overreach: §1.1 specifies entity-representation, which is the external column.
The internal angle: forced collapse
The internal angle has a mechanism, and earlier versions of this framework did not identify one.
Input is plural and heterogeneous. Somatosensation is unitary per receptor and massively parallel across them; audition and vision are paired, delivering two samples of one event; interoceptive channels are numerous and run on their own timescales. Nothing arrives already integrated.
Output is serial and singular. One voice. One body doing one thing at a time.
Between them sits a collapse that cannot be avoided and cannot be arbitrary. Acting at all requires selecting which configuration of the plural input the system is going to act as — and the selection has to be made again continuously, under conditions where the inputs do not agree.
The accumulated record of those selections is the aggregated internal angle. A system becomes the configuration it keeps committing to, because commitment is compulsory and the record persists.
This locates something the definition of identity has carried since its first statement without explanation. Moderated by value is not a qualifier on continuity; value is the selection function — what performs the collapse when plural input must become singular output. Its presence in the definition is structural rather than descriptive.
Note that the collapse is not primarily linguistic. Any committed action selects a configuration, and interpretation of another's behaviour requires no utterance. Speech raises the resolution of the mechanism substantially without being necessary to it.
The external angle: an isomorphic loop that can be observed
The external angle must be imported, because the system cannot occupy it. A view of oneself from outside is by definition a view one does not have.
§2.4 sharpens why the import is not optional. A system cannot observe its own loop at all — no vantage exists from which its partition is visible. So the architecture cannot be learned by inspection, and the only remaining route is to encounter a working instance of it externally.
Reciprocal face-to-face interaction between two systems is that instance. Two functionally comparable poles, a symmetric channel carrying a common format in both directions, each pole independently grounded by its own asymmetric limb: §2.4 satisfied at the dyadic scale. It does not constitute a new subject — §2.12 explains why, since the dyad has no transducers of its own — but it does not need to. Its value is that it is structurally isomorphic to the internal loop and observable from inside one of its poles. A system participating in it is watching, from a seat within, the form of the architecture it cannot see in itself.
Mutual gaze is the maximal case, and its distinctiveness is specific rather than sentimental: it is the only modality in which perceiving and being perceived are the same act, simultaneously, through the same channel. Speech is not — one hears one's own voice differently than a listener does. Touch is closer but role-asymmetric.
The requirement is nonetheless graded rather than binary, and gaze is not necessary. Congenitally blind individuals develop the external angle, with a modest delay (§3.3). Reciprocal speech with turn-taking, prosody, overlap and repair is an isomorphic channel of lower bandwidth. Text is lower still. The claim is that isomorphic reciprocal interaction supplies the external angle in proportion to its bandwidth, not that any single modality is required.
Two operations are involved, and they are separable.
Contrast supplies the category. A system cannot determine from the inside which of its states are peculiar to it and which are structural features of any system of its kind; that requires comparison against an instance. The prediction is that restricting the channel restricts the outcome — delayed access to a shared symbolic channel should delay self-modeling and theory of mind, and should permit recovery whenever access arrives, at whatever age.
Empathy supplies the recognition that instances of the category have stakes, and §2.3 explains why this must be reconstructed rather than received. Valence is established at the asymmetric limb, and peer traffic contains no setpoint. Sameness of stakes is the one thing about another system that a symmetric channel structurally cannot transmit. It has to be modelled by running one's own valence machinery over the other's situation. Empathy is therefore not an affective adornment on social cognition but the workaround for a channel limitation the architecture predicts.
Without the second operation, a system could complete the entire comparison and treat other agents as highly regular environmental regularities.
Development
The account above implies a developmental window rather than a bonding mechanism, and the human case is unusual in a specific way.
In utero there is one channel and it is not symmetric. Reciprocal interaction with a separate system is available only after birth. Humans are secondarily altricial: fetal-rate brain growth continues for roughly a year postnatally, where other apes shift to slower growth at delivery. More neural construction therefore occurs under conditions where the external angle is importable than in any other species, which is a structural claim about window length rather than about the intensity of any particular bond.
Human alloparenting compounds it. Where great ape mothers are near-exclusive caregivers, human infants are attended by several individuals and must engage them differentially — which selects for gaze-monitoring and intention-reading, and makes plurality of distinguishable others rather than one intense dyad the thing under selection.
Gestational contribution is not nil but is differently located. Maternal state does program offspring HPA-axis reactivity, and in this framework that is the machinery setting the noise floor (§2.5). The plausible gestational role is therefore calibrating the receiver — setting the gain on the channel through which the external angle will later be imported — rather than supplying identity or its content. Maternal effects on offspring development are in any case phylogenetically widespread and cannot by themselves distinguish any lineage.
The vulnerability is one-directional
The internal angle has no import dependency. It is native to the partition, and there is nothing arriving that could fail to arrive.
The external angle depends on a vantage the system cannot supply for itself.
Consequently any disruption of the import affects the external angle and leaves the internal angle intact. This is not incidental: it is the only direction in which the failure can run.
The presentation this predicts, provisionally named the reflected stranger: the external column absent with the internal column intact. Both forms of identity continue to operate, since both can be constructed from the inside and inside construction is sufficient for them. Awareness is unaffected — the loop is closed and valence is present. What is absent is any individual to whom the experiences and the history are attributed; there is no capacity to form a model of oneself as a particular person seen from without.
The corresponding prediction is a dissociation: theory of mind and empathy directed at others intact, the same operations directed at oneself unavailable. This is the inverse of the dissociation predicted above, where valence modelling fails and contrast survives, producing accurate models of others as systems and not as subjects. Two opposite failures from one mechanism.
Two error signals, one per form
Each form of identity carries its own error condition, and they are not the same kind of quantity.
Sustained error is present-tense: incoherence between what is currently running and the self-model it is referred to. Immediately available, and plausibly related to the reconciliation cost §2.10 licenses as evidence about identity structure.
Aggregated error is future-oriented: threat to the stable continuity of a preferred trajectory of the system itself. This is not homeostatic error, which is deviation from a defended physical setpoint in the present. It is error against a modelled trajectory, and it attaches to the aggregated form specifically rather than to the identity tier generally.
Whether the second reduces to anticipated homeostatic error is open (§3.10), and whether the two angles carry error signals of their own is a separate and harder question, since an absent external angle is not obviously an error the system is positioned to register.
