Verified: Katlowitz et al., Nature 654:714–723 (2026), DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-10448-0; Filipchuk, Bathellier and Destexhe, Nature Neuroscience (2022); geniculate synapse proportions (Van Horn et al., 2000; Sherman and Guillery, 2002); Pinto et al., Brain 140:1231–1237 (2017) and the Volz–Gazzaniga cross-cueing response; Bodien et al., NEJM 391:598–608 (2024); Sarasso et al., Current Biology (2015) and Sanders et al. (2012) on connected and disconnected states; Schiff (2010) on the mesocircuit; Williams et al., eLife 2:e01157 (2013); Kliemann et al., Cell Reports (2019) on hemispherectomy; the Waterman deafferentation case (McNeill et al., 2010); the REM aminergic/cholinergic profile.
Unverified: Moruzzi and Magoun on tegmental lesions, and the anesthetic hysteresis literature invoked in §2.5. The latter carries argumentative weight and should be verified first. Full page-level citations are outstanding for the callosal agenesis literature, the Ross et al. (1984) mutism report in §3.4, and Knecht et al. (2000, 2001) on lateralization in §2.5 and §3.7. Newly added and unverified: Hrdy on alloparenting and the delphinid signature-whistle literature, both in §2.8; the megapode and crocodilian parental-care distribution in §3.3.
Verified in this cycle, with one correction that matters.
Deaf theory-of-mind delay (§2.8) — confirmed. Peterson & Siegal, Child Development (2002): deaf late signers show ToM deficits relative to deaf native signers and hearing controls, surviving controls for syntax, spatial mental age and executive function, with younger native signers outperforming older late signers. Neural corroboration in Richardson et al., Nature Communications (2020): reduced ToM-selectivity in RTPJ with delayed sign exposure. Recovery on entry to a signing peer community is reported and supports the framework's "at whatever age" clause. One complication to note: at least one study of deaf college students found deficits unrelated to early access, which the developmental reading does not straightforwardly accommodate.
Blind theory-of-mind delay (§2.8) — confirmed but substantially smaller than earlier drafts implied. Older work reported 4–7 year delays; Brambring & Asbrock (2010), using tasks minimising visual components, found 19–26 months resolving by age seven, and two further studies found no delay. Bedny and colleagues additionally report that growing up blind does not alter the neural bases of ToM. Earlier drafts of this framework cited the larger figures and should not. The corrected magnitude is in fact the better fit: deaf children of non-signing parents have the channel itself degraded, blind children have full linguistic access missing one component, and the framework predicts the observed ordering. That prediction is post-hoc and is offered as consistency rather than support.
Cognitive/affective empathy dissociation (§2.8) — confirmed, and dimension-specific rather than general. The pattern traces to Cleckley's emotion paradox and has meta-analytic support for intact explicit cognitive processing of others' distress alongside impaired affective responsivity at the automatic-visceral level. But it does not hold across psychopathy uniformly: meanness and primary psychopathy show broad impairment, disinhibition and secondary psychopathy show diminished cognitive empathy, and boldness shows the specific profile the framework predicts — enhanced cognitive empathy with reduced affective empathy. §2.8 should cite primary and callous-affective traits, not psychopathy as a category.
Avian pallial convergence (§3.3) — confirmed. Stacho et al., Science 369:eabc5534 (2020).
Alexithymia and interoception (§2.3) — prediction not supported. v2.1.0 predicted alexithymia as degraded establishment with intact uptake, tracking objective interoceptive accuracy. Trevisan et al. (2019), across 66 samples and N ≈ 7,146, find only a small negative association with interoceptive awareness overall, with strength and direction depending heavily on which component is measured and how: the association holds for self-reported accuracy and not for objective accuracy, and the authors note the former may reflect shared method variance. A separate meta-analysis across ten studies found a weak negative correlation with interoceptive sensibility and none with objective accuracy, and the original heartbeat-tracking association has failed to replicate in several attempts. The 2024 systematic review reports alexithymia associated positively with interoceptive confusion, heightened interoceptive attention, and autonomic reactivity. The predicted direction is therefore wrong, and the observed pattern is one the framework cannot currently sort. §2.3 records this rather than reframing it, and §3.10.11 states what accommodating it would cost.
On the state of the evidence, for a reader assessing this cold. Four claims have now been checked at source. Two confirmed as stated, one confirmed with the magnitude substantially corrected downward, one failed. The failed one was among the framework's more recent and more confident predictions. A reader should weight the remaining unverified items accordingly, and should treat any section describing itself as newly derived with more suspicion than its prose invites.
Recorded so it is not re-derived: maternal-fetal microchimerism. Fetal cells persist in maternal tissue for decades and maternal cells in offspring, which superficially bears on §2.3's boundary claim. It is documented broadly across placental mammals with no established cognitive association, and it is the kind of fact that reads as confirmation while supplying none. Not used.
§2.3 and §2.4 remain load-bearing. §2.2 establishes a necessary condition that is widely satisfied; attacks on it do not reach the framework. §2.3 has grown substantially across v2.0.0 and v2.1.0 — constraint efficacy, the setpoint amendment, and now the establishment/uptake decomposition — and its load-bearing status has increased accordingly. The decomposition is the newest material in a section the framework cannot afford to have wrong, and it was reached quickly. It earns its place by sorting the clinical dissociations more precisely than the undecomposed account, and that is the property to attack if attacking it.
§2.12 is among the newest material and the least tested. It was introduced in response to a met falsification condition, which is structurally the shape of an ad-hoc rescue. §3.8 states its disposal test explicitly.
§2.8's two halves do not have equal standing and should not be read as though they do. The two-forms split — sustained and aggregated — rests on an argument from independent error conditions and on its accounting for the tier's known failure modes. The two-angles split rests on that plus a single first-person report from a reporter who knew the framework, and §3.8 carries its disposal test. A reader should press hardest on the angles.
§2.9's standing lock is not decorative. The identity tier now decomposes into four cells, and a decomposition of selfhood invites exactly one misuse. The lock is stated in the text so that the misuse requires contradicting the document rather than merely extending it.
The tier separation is stipulative and will be misread. §1.1 carries the whole burden of preventing it. The identity-tier material in §2.8 increases the load on that section rather than relieving it.
On the provenance of the revisions. Substantial portions of v2.0.0 derive from cross-reading this framework against a companion project on epistemic governance for agent memory. Convergence between the two is weak evidence, since both are the work of one author in one period against overlapping intuitions, and this should not be presented as independent corroboration. The items worth weighting are those that produced disanalogies or new falsifiable predictions rather than further matches.
